<title>SWEDEN: EMU could mean change to Swedish Riksbank board.</title>
<headline>EMU could mean change to Swedish Riksbank board.</headline>
<dateline>STOCKHOLM 1997-02-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The make-up of Sweden's central bank board may have to change if Sweden joins Europe's economic and monetary union, the central bank's deputy governor Lars Heikensten said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>Active members of parliament currently sit on the Riksbank board.</p>
<p>&quot;Active parliamentary members are against traditions existing in central Europe,&quot; Heikensten said in a speech.</p>
<p>According to the Maastricht Treaty, the central bank also has to be made more independent, Heikensten said.</p>
<p>&quot;This means the Riksbank governor must have a clearer position,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>&quot;It should only be possible to dismiss him or her if there are strong reasons such as severe mismanagement.&quot;</p>
<p>The Riksbank should not be able to receive directives from others, apart from laws passed by, for example, the government, Heikensten said.</p>
<p>-- Birgitta Forsberg, Stockholm newsroom +46-8-700 1006</p>
